\chapter{Portability and release evidence}
\label{ch:117}
\index{Portability and release evidence}

Release results are specific to compiler revision, source dependencies, configuration, toolchain, operating system and target. Some On1x features run on ordinary native C11, while the cooperative runtime, process captures, certain native modules, and the isolated plugin worker rely on documented host profiles. Illustrative API availability and measured portability are different data.

\section{Construct a release matrix}
Start with the generated and fallback scanners, direct C11 execution, and the supported MIR scalar subset. Add opt-in integrations only where their dependencies can be pinned and built. Label compiler checks, execution tests, sanitizers, and relocation tests distinctly. Include failure behavior for unsupported operations on each target. A recorded skip should say what capability was absent. When recording an unrelated inventory failure, identify its test and verify whether the same failure occurs on the base checkout. Avoid reusing past totals for a release assembled from different sources.

\section{Interpret a native toolchain probe}
For a native Linux x86-64/glibc row, the toolchain probe takes three
separately named executables: the C driver, assembler and linker. Its
first question is whether the driver reports the intended target.
Its C translation unit then checks layout assumptions; a separate
assembly routine supplies the other half of a calling-convention test.
Inspecting ELF headers catches a wrong object class or machine before
execution. Running the linked fixture then checks something the headers
cannot: an integer and a double must actually arrive in the expected
registers, and a double result must come back intact.

Suppose the C compilation and assembly steps succeed but the final
driver link cannot find compatible startup objects. The truthful row
records the link-stage failure and no successful probe record. Marking
it ``compiler works'' would conceal the very condition that prevents
running the application. Conversely, on success the JSON identifies
the three tools by path, version banner and digest. A repeated probe
on unchanged inputs can be compared byte for byte because temporary
paths and timestamps are excluded.

What remains outside that comparison? The startup objects, headers,
system libraries and dynamic loader participate in execution, but
their contents are not all pinned by the three tool hashes. Nor did
the probe produce a shared object, cross-run a foreign machine, or
exercise a MuLIB callback. Put those questions in separate rows. The
verification record's GCC repeatability result and its focused Clang
run are useful exactly because they state this limited native profile.

